# PAST_EXAM_ENHANCEMENT — Feature Build Guide (Legacy App)

This document specifies how to build the **PAST_EXAM_ENHANCEMENT** feature for the Safegloss legacy Django app.

Goal: For any indexed course (the same ones supported by your **Standards / Learning Objectives** feature), the system can:

1) **Find reliable sources** for past exams and exam resources (official or reputable),
2) **Acquire and store** exam papers, mark schemes, sample responses, scoring rubrics, and metadata,
3) **Parse** the materials into structured, item-level data,
4) Use **AI-assisted tagging** to map each exam item to:
   - one or more learning objectives (from your standards database), and
   - cognitive/action verbs (publisher-native where possible; otherwise mapped to an internal taxonomy like Bloom),
5) Let teachers **browse and search** past exams down to the item level.

---

## 1) Scope and Non-Negotiables

### In scope
- Past exams and exam prep resources: papers, item banks (where permitted), mark schemes, rubrics, examiner reports, sample/anchor responses.
- Course alignment: only for courses you can identify via **Authority → Program → Subject → Grade/Course**.
- Item-level browsing and metadata.
- Provenance and licensing awareness.

### Out of scope (v1)
- Automatically generating new exam items.
- Student-facing practice mode (v1 is teacher/admin browsing and analytics).

### Non-negotiables
- **Provenance**: Every artifact and item must record *where it came from* and *how it was obtained*.
- **Licensing**: Some publishers restrict redistribution. The pipeline must support **reference-only** and **teacher-provided upload** modes.
- **Native hierarchy preservation**: If an exam paper has official numbering/sections, preserve it.

---

## 2) User Experience (Teacher-Only)

### Entry points
- Teacher page: `/teacher/exams/`
- From a course/standards page: “View Past Exams” button.

### Single-page filter UI (matches standards filter style)
Teacher selects:
- Authority (multi-select)
- Program/Framework (multi-select)
- Subject
- Course / Grade level
- Session/series (if applicable): e.g., May/June, Oct/Nov, Spring, etc.
- Year range
- Paper/component (if applicable)
- Item filters: format, difficulty/cognitive level, rubric criterion, objective code (native/internal)

### Results
- List of exam documents (papers) with:
  - Source panel (publisher, official link, acquisition method)
  - Publication/administration date (if known)
  - Coverage summary: number of items, components, available mark schemes, sample responses

### Drill-down
- Paper → Section → Question → Sub-question
- For each item:
  - prompt + any provided stimulus material
  - mark value / weight
  - mark scheme excerpt and/or rubric
  - sample responses (if available)
  - AI tags:
    - mapped learning objectives (with confidence)
    - action verbs / cognitive level
    - item format + descriptors

---

## 3) Data Model (Core Entities)

Design the exam system similarly to standards: versioned, provenance-first, with an ingestion pipeline.

### 3.1 Authority alignment
All exams must link to the same course identity used by standards:
- Authority → Program → Subject → Grade/Course (and optionally a unique course code)

### 3.2 Models (recommended)

**ExamAuthority**
- This should typically reuse your existing `Authority` + `AuthorityProgram` models.

**CourseOffering** (or reuse your course table)
- Represents the course context you index (authority_program + subject + grade/course).

**ExamSource**
- `name` (publisher/organization/site name)
- `source_type` (enum: `official_publisher`, `government`, `testing_org`, `reputable_repository`, `teacher_upload`)
- `base_url`
- `reliability_rating` (enum: `high`, `medium`, `low`)
- `notes`

**ExamArtifact** (raw evidence snapshot)
- FK `course_offering`
- FK `exam_source`
- `artifact_type` (enum: `paper_pdf`, `mark_scheme_pdf`, `rubric_pdf`, `examiner_report_pdf`, `sample_responses_pdf`, `html_page`, `zip_bundle`, `image_set`, `other`)
- `source_url` (nullable if teacher upload)
- `retrieved_at`
- `content_type` (pdf/html/zip/...)
- `file_path` or storage key
- `sha256_raw`
- `license_notes`
- `acquisition_method` (enum; see Section 4)

**ExamDocument** (structured “paper”)
- FK `course_offering`
- `title`
- `exam_year`
- `exam_session` (string; e.g., “May/June”, “Oct/Nov”, “Spring”)
- `paper_code` / `component_code` (string)
- `administration_date` (date, nullable)
- `published_at` (date, nullable)
- `version_label` (string)
- `source_url` (official link)
- `source_accessed_at`
- `source_publisher_name`
- `source_content_type`
- `acquisition_method`
- `evidence_artifact_id` (FK ExamArtifact)
- `checksum_canonical` (sha256 of canonical JSON)

**ExamNode** (tree; paper → sections → items)
- FK `exam_document`
- `parent` self-FK nullable
- `node_type` (enum: `paper`, `section`, `question`, `part`, `subpart`, `stimulus`, `table`, `figure`)
- `native_code` (e.g., “Q1(a)(i)”)
- `internal_code` (e.g., “1.1.1”) — must preserve native hierarchy
- `title` (optional)
- `prompt_text` (for question/part)
- `stimulus_ref` (optional link to stored stimulus node)
- `marks` (numeric, nullable)
- `sort_order`

**ExamScoringGuide**
- FK `exam_document`
- `guide_type` (enum: `mark_scheme`, `rubric`, `scoring_notes`)
- FK `evidence_artifact_id`
- `text` (structured or plain; keep references)

**ExamItemAnnotation** (AI + human tags)
- FK `exam_node` (must be node_type question/part/subpart)
- FK `standards_document` (or authority_program + version) (nullable)
- `objective_ids` (M2M to ObjectiveNode via join table recommended)
- `objective_match_method` (enum: `ai`, `manual`, `hybrid`)
- `objective_confidence` (float)
- `action_verb_native` (string, nullable)
- `action_verb_internal` (string; e.g., Bloom category)
- `cognitive_level_internal` (enum: `remember`, `understand`, `apply`, `analyze`, `evaluate`, `create`)
- `item_format` (enum: `mcq`, `short_answer`, `essay`, `practical`, `oral`, `data_response`, `matching`, `true_false`, `other`)
- `meta` (JSON for extensible descriptors: criterion, strand, topic, calculator_allowed, etc.)
- `created_at`, `updated_at`

**ExamSampleResponse**
- FK `exam_node`
- `response_type` (enum: `anchor`, `exemplar`, `candidate_response`, `commentary`)
- FK `evidence_artifact_id`
- `text`
- `score_awarded` (nullable)
- `rater_notes` (nullable)

> Design note: keep exam text and scoring content as close to the original as licensing permits. When restricted, store references (links + codes) and only minimal derived metadata.

